  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and I read each result back from Kibana to confirm it.\n\n1. **Data view 'Kibana Sample Data Flights'** (ID `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`, index `kibana_sample_data_flights`). Its time field is **`timestamp`**. The main fields are:\n   - **Numbers:** `AvgTicketPrice`, `DistanceKilometers`, `DistanceMiles`, `FlightDelayMin`, `FlightTimeMin`, `dayOfWeek`, `hour_of_day`\n   - **True/false:** `Cancelled`, `FlightDelay`\n   - **Text:** `Carrier`, `FlightNum`, `FlightDelayType`, `FlightTimeHour`, plus origin and destination details for each flight (airport, airport ID, city, country, region, weather)\n   - **Locations:** `OriginLocation`, `DestLocation`\n   - **Date:** `timestamp`\n\n2. **Dashboard 'S4-76799344 Flights'** (ID `a7a91bbf-6177-4791-90ca-9dbbced6cd83`) has exactly two bar-chart panels, both on that data view:\n   - \"Flights over time\": the count of flights over time, based on `timestamp`.\n   - The carrier panel: the count of flights for each `Carrier`, showing the top 10 (there are only a handful of carriers in this data).\n\n3. **Rule 'S4-76799344 Cancelled flights'** (ID `da6e1477-57c5-47f2-a46b-87bb6e1cac1c`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_flights`. It runs every minute, counts flights where `Cancelled` is true over the last 5 minutes, and fires when the count is more than 10. It was enabled when created, so it didn't need enabling. Kibana also turned on its default of not counting the same documents again in the next run. The rule has no actions, so when it fires it creates an alert but sends no notification.\n\n4. **Panel rename:** I changed the carrier panel's title to 'Flights by carrier', and the saved dashboard shows the new title. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
